# Copy to .env and adjust. Every value below is the built-in default.
#
# In production (Docker Swarm — see docker-stack.yml), the credential-bearing
# values here (DATABASE_URL, APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY, SESSION_SECRET,
# ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD, SMTP_PASSWORD, TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL) can each be
# supplied via Docker secrets instead: set e.g. APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY_FILE to a
# mounted secret file's path (typically /run/secrets/<name>) and leave
# APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY itself unset — the app reads the file's contents at
# boot. A real environment variable always wins over its _FILE counterpart
# if both are somehow set. This .env file is still the right way to run
# locally; _FILE is purely a Swarm/production concern.

APP_ENV=development
HTTP_ADDR=:8080

DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/ssl_generator?sslmode=disable
DB_MAX_CONNS=10
DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=10s

# How often the background sweeper re-evaluates every issued certificate's
# expiry against the thresholds below. This is the only thing left on a
# timer — there's no live TLS handshake to schedule any more, just a cheap
# scan of certificates already on file.
ALERT_SWEEP_INTERVAL=12h

# A certificate becomes "expiring", then "critical", then a last-notice alert
# this many days before notAfter. These also gate email/Teams alerts (below).
#
# v1.5: these three are now portal-editable from /settings (admin/editor) —
# these env values are only a SEED, used the very first time the app boots
# against a fresh database. After that first boot, /settings and the
# database are authoritative and these lines are ignored; edit them there,
# not here. Leave them here (or empty) if you're fine setting them from the
# portal after first boot instead.
EXPIRY_WARNING_DAYS=30
EXPIRY_CRITICAL_DAYS=7
EXPIRY_FINAL_DAYS=1

# Percent-of-total-lifetime-remaining counterparts to the two thresholds
# above — whichever of (days remaining, percent of lifetime remaining) trips
# a tier first is what's used. These only ever apply to *externally*-issued
# certificates (uploaded/attached from an outside CA), since those are the
# ones actually bound by the CA/Browser Forum's shrinking public
# max-validity schedule (200 days today, 100 from March 2027, 47 from March
# 2029) — a shorter total validity should mean earlier relative warning.
# Internal certificates (self-signed, or signed by a Root CA this app
# holds) set their own validity period, often years, so a percentage of it
# isn't a meaningful urgency signal and is deliberately ignored for them —
# see domain.Certificate.HealthStatus's doc comment for the full reasoning.
# 33/10 means: an external certificate down to a third of its life
# remaining is "expiring"; down to a tenth is "critical".
EXPIRY_WARNING_PERCENT=33
EXPIRY_CRITICAL_PERCENT=10

# Alerting — email and/or Microsoft Teams. Fires once per state change (a
# certificate crossing a threshold), not on every sweep. Leave a channel's
# settings empty to disable just that channel; leave both empty and alerting
# logs a boot-time warning and stays fully off — nothing else about the app
# depends on it being configured.
#
# v1.5: every SMTP/Teams setting below is now portal-editable from
# /settings (admin/editor) — these env values are only a first-boot SEED,
# exactly like EXPIRY_WARNING_DAYS above. Edit them from the portal after
# first boot, not here.

# SMTP — leave SMTP_HOST empty to disable email alerts. SMTP_HOST and
# ALERT_EMAIL_FROM also double as the transport a ticket's "send by email"
# button uses to send a certificate straight to its requester — that only
# needs these two set, not ALERT_EMAIL_TO below (which is just the alert
# distribution list).
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USERNAME=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
ALERT_EMAIL_FROM=
# Comma-separated list of recipients for expiry alerts specifically — unused
# by ticket send-by-email, which emails whoever the admin/editor typed in.
ALERT_EMAIL_TO=

# Microsoft Teams incoming webhook URL — leave empty to disable Teams alerts.
TEAMS_WEBHOOK_URL=

# 32 random bytes, base64 encoded — encrypts stored private keys with AES-256-GCM.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Leave empty to store private keys as plaintext PEM (development only).
#
# v1.5: unlike every other setting in this file, this is NOT a plain seed —
# it's the first-boot value for the vault's own encryption key, from then on
# stored (and portal-editable, with automatic re-encryption of every stored
# private key) in app_settings. Change it after first boot only from
# /settings's "Rotate encryption key" danger-zone form, never by editing
# this file and restarting — that would leave every already-stored private
# key encrypted under a key the app no longer has. See CLAUDE.md's locked
# decision on this for the full reasoning and the security tradeoff of this
# key now living in the same database it protects.
APP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# 32 random bytes, base64 encoded — signs the short-lived "password verified,
# awaiting MFA" token. Required once any account exists; `make env` generates
# one automatically.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# How long a signed-in session survives without activity, and its hard cap
# regardless of activity.
SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT=12h
SESSION_ABSOLUTE_TIMEOUT=720h

# Mark the session/CSRF cookies Secure (HTTPS-only). Leave false for local
# HTTP development; set true once the app sits behind TLS.
COOKIE_SECURE=false

# Bootstraps the very first admin account when the users table is empty —
# ignored once any account exists. Required for the first login after a
# clean `docker compose up`; `make env` generates a random password so a
# fresh environment is usable out of the box. Sign in and change it
# immediately — the account is forced to anyway.
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD=

# Certificate request tickets (v1.2 self-service workflow): how many days
# after approval an open ticket is flagged overdue on the ticket queue.
# Confirmed at 3 days for both internal and external tickets.
#
# v1.5: portal-editable from /settings — this is only a first-boot seed,
# same as EXPIRY_WARNING_DAYS above.
TICKET_SLA_DAYS=3

# v1.3: how often a background sweeper drafts a pending renewal ticket, on
# the original requester's behalf, for an internal certificate approaching
# expiry (same expiry thresholds as EXPIRY_WARNING_DAYS/PERCENT above) — see
# CertificateRequestService.AutoDraftRenewals. Scoped to internal
# certificates only; external renewal stays a deliberate admin action.
# Leave empty to disable this entirely and keep the app purely reactive.
RENEWAL_SWEEP_INTERVAL=12h

# v1.3: DigiCert renewal integration (Phase 6) — lets an admin submit an
# in-progress external renewal ticket straight to DigiCert's CertCentral API
# instead of pasting a certificate a CA issued outside the app by hand. The
# manual paste flow is always available regardless of this setting. Leave
# DIGICERT_API_KEY empty to disable the integration entirely.
# DIGICERT_BASE_URL is required once DIGICERT_API_KEY is set — the value
# below is DigiCert's documented CertCentral v2 base URL as of this writing;
# confirm it against dev.digicert.com before relying on it in production
# (see internal/pkg/digicert's package doc comment for what's unverified).
DIGICERT_API_KEY=
DIGICERT_BASE_URL=https://www.digicert.com/services/v2

# ADCS (Active Directory Certificate Services) CES/CEP integration — a third
# "sign with" option on the pending-certificate detail page, alongside
# self-sign and an in-app Root CA. Leave ADCS_ENDPOINT empty to disable it
# entirely. ADCS_ENDPOINT is the full URL of ADCS's Certificate Enrollment
# Web Service configured for username/password authentication (get this from
# your ADCS admin console, not guessed at) — a typical shape looks like
# https://adcs.corp.example.com/ADPolicyProvider_CEP_UsernamePassword/service.svc/CES,
# not a bare hostname. ADCS_USERNAME/ADCS_PASSWORD/ADCS_TEMPLATE become
# required once ADCS_ENDPOINT is set — the account needs Enroll permission on
# the named certificate template. See internal/pkg/adcs and CLAUDE.md's ADCS
# section for what is and isn't verified against a real server.
#
# v1.16: every ADCS_* setting below is now portal-editable from /settings
# (admin-only) — these env values are only a first-boot SEED, exactly like
# the LDAP_* settings above. A saved change on the portal takes effect on
# the very next submission, no restart needed. Edit ADCS configuration from
# the portal after first boot, not here.
ADCS_ENDPOINT=
ADCS_USERNAME=
ADCS_PASSWORD=
ADCS_TEMPLATE=

# v1.4: LDAP authentication. Leave LDAP_URL empty to keep every account on a
# local password exactly as before this feature existed. Once set: an admin
# account still always signs in with a local password, but every other
# account authenticates via LDAP (search-then-bind) instead — a first-time
# login auto-creates ("JIT-provisions") the local account record, and every
# login re-syncs its role from LDAP group membership. LDAP_BIND_DN/
# LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD/LDAP_BASE_DN/LDAP_USER_FILTER/LDAP_GROUP_FILTER become
# required once LDAP_URL is set. See CLAUDE.md's LDAP locked decisions for
# the full authentication model, including why this can lock a pre-existing
# non-admin local account out of its password once LDAP is turned on.
#
# v1.8: every LDAP_* setting below is now portal-editable from /settings
# (admin-only) — these env values are only a first-boot SEED, exactly like
# EXPIRY_WARNING_DAYS/SMTP_HOST above. A saved change on the portal takes
# effect on the very next login, no restart needed. Edit LDAP configuration
# from the portal after first boot, not here.
LDAP_URL=
# Service account used to search the directory — never an end user's own
# credentials. LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD is Docker-secrets-eligible (see
# secretFileKeys in internal/config/config.go).
LDAP_BIND_DN=cn=svc-sslgen,ou=service-accounts,dc=example,dc=com
LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD=
# Search base for both the user lookup and the group-membership lookup.
LDAP_BASE_DN=dc=example,dc=com
# Finds a user's entry by the email they submit at login — exactly one %s
# placeholder.
LDAP_USER_FILTER=(mail=%s)
# Finds every group a user's entry belongs to — exactly one %s placeholder,
# standing in for the user's DN.
LDAP_GROUP_FILTER=(&(objectClass=groupOfNames)(member=%s))
# Semicolon-separated LDAP group DNs whose members are provisioned with (and
# re-synced to) that role — checked in this order, so a user in more than
# one mapped group gets the most privileged matching role. Semicolon, not
# comma, since a DN's own RDN components are already comma-separated (e.g.
# "cn=a,ou=b,dc=c"). Leaving all three empty while LDAP_URL is set is a
# configuration error (every LDAP login would be denied and logged with
# nowhere to map to). There is deliberately no admin mapping — admin is
# always a locally-created account, never LDAP-derived.
# Example with two groups mapped to the same role:
# LDAP_ROLE_MAP_EDITOR=cn=sslgen-editors,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com;cn=platform-team,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
LDAP_ROLE_MAP_EDITOR=cn=sslgen-editors,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
LDAP_ROLE_MAP_VIEWER=cn=sslgen-viewers,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
LDAP_ROLE_MAP_REQUESTER=cn=sslgen-requesters,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com
